<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Romagna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Romagna is a historical region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a>, the Adriatic and the lower Po, now the eastern part of Emilia-Romagna. Its towns, among them Ravenna, Rimini, Forlì, Faenza and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesena">Cesena</a>, were held by local lords under papal overlordship until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a> and Julius II brought them under direct control in the early 16th century. Part of the Papal States until 1859, it voted in 1860 to join the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and so became part of Italy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romagna.jpg" alt="Romagna" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Erinaceus · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Romagna_geopolitica.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
